  "summary": "The existing ideation correctly identifies the decomposition axes and sequencing trap around modals, but overlooks a practical incremental approach that exploits the file's natural seams. The 4,298-line tui.py has five clear extraction layers visible in the line ranges: (1) Lines 84-481 are pure standalone functions (_feature_markdown, _escape_markup, _format_json_text, _build_feature_detail_widgets, handle_cli_commands, _handle_config_command, _handle_context_command) with zero PipelineApp coupling — these can move to a tui_helpers.py immediately with a single import change. (2) Lines 482-568 (StageHeader, FeatureItem) are simple Static widgets that only depend on FeatureFile data — extractable to tui_widgets.py. (3) Lines 569-1457 are 11 modal classes — but the previous ideation is right that these should NOT be extracted first because AnswerQuestionsModal, UnifiedEditModal, RunScriptModal, and CreateScriptModal all receive complex objects and their dismiss() return values drive async workflows in PipelineApp. (4) Lines 1458-1903 (SettingsPane, ScriptsPane, AgentStatusWidget) are sidebar components with moderate coupling to PipelineApp reactive state. (5) Lines 1904-4262 is PipelineApp itself with ~40 action methods plus compose/mount/refresh logic. The recommended first PR should extract layer (1) — the ~400 lines of pure helper functions — which is a mechanical move with zero design decisions. The second PR should extract the lock management and entry point (lines 4263-4298) into a tui_main.py. For the action_* split, rather than a mixin or declarative table (which fight Textual's action binding model), the cleanest approach is to group them into a tui_actions.py module as free async functions that accept a PipelineApp instance — this avoids multiple inheritance complexity while still reducing the monolith. The _check_for_changes() scaling issue should use a hash-based dirty check (hash the sorted list of file paths + mtimes into a single comparison value) rather than introducing inotify/watchdog dependencies, since the current polling interval is already coarse-grained and the real cost is the per-file stat calls being redundantly processed, not the stat syscalls themselves."
